+\subsection{\textcolor{blue}{Certification Statistics and Reproducibility}}
+\label{app:results_statistics}
+
+\textcolor{blue}{
+    \textbf{Certification statistics.} The 42 single-scenario evaluations of the certification campaign are evaluations 3--11, 15, 22, 23, 26--31, 34--39 and 42--59 of Table~\ref{tab:appE_evaluations}. Of these, 32 certified, 24 on the oscillatory branch and 8 on the monotone branch; the 10 uncertified divide into 5 refused by the gap clause, 2 reset by residual lapses and 3 failed by the growth test. The median certification cycle was 174, and certification came a median 65 cycles after the first residual pass (range 21--89); the median ratio of the window range to $\tau$ at certification was 0.86. The four evaluations added later (32, 33, 60 and 61) all certified. Ten of the 46 certificates the tables use stop within 5\,\% of $\tau$ (evaluations 2, 3, 5, 10, 11, 12, 14, 17, 20 and 23; evaluation~36, at 0.94 on the monotone branch, is just below), so the rule bounds the stopping error of each evaluation at about $\tau$, not well inside it. The two evaluations continued past their certificates, 14 and 3, moved by a further 0.74$\tau$ and 0.78--0.88$\tau$. After the first residual pass, 21 cycles in 10 evaluations had a local solve that was not clean, all in transmission blocks (column N.c.), while the 119 exits at IPOPT's acceptable level within ten times the tight-tail tolerances, all in distribution blocks, were counted as clean.
+}
+% [CONFIRM -- W179] the 46 certificates = the 49 certified rows of Table E.2 less evaluation 16 (superseded) and the two
+% bitwise twins (56, 61); the ten >= 0.95 tau = at_or_above_0.95_tau_counted; continued cells b_4649234b (0.738 tau),
+% d_c52e1670 (0.781 / 0.882 tau) per paragraphs_v2 (ii); 119 acceptable-clean events after N, all DSO (W153).
+
+\textcolor{blue}{
+    \textbf{Reproducibility.} Replaying a run reproduces its trajectory bitwise: the multi-scenario evaluation of the plan without storage replayed its 72 recorded cycles bitwise; the three single-scenario reference continuations replayed bitwise to their certification cycles; and every evaluation repeated from an earlier record replayed that record bitwise up to its first residual pass before continuing (column Replay). The evaluations ran one at a time on an Apple M2~Max with 32~GiB of memory under macOS~27.0, single-threaded: OMP, MKL, OpenBLAS, vecLib and NumExpr were capped at one thread in every evaluation's recorded environment, and the HSL library is built without OpenMP, so MA97 ran serially; the solver settings and versions are listed in~\ref{app:admm_updated_settings}. Two distribution blocks (node~7 in 2025 Winter, node~5 in 2035 Winter) end some primary solves at IPOPT's acceptable level within ten times the tight-tail tolerances; they are counted as clean under the rule of~\ref{app:admm_updated_certification}. Two offsets are common to every evaluation and cancel in every reported difference: tightening the interior-point tail lowered the certified objective of each of the three single-scenario references by $1.0$--$1.2 \times 10^{-6}$ relative, with the same sign (the complementarity tolerance went to $10^{-6}$ from $10^{-4}$ in the distribution subproblems and from $5 \times 10^{-4}$ in the transmission subproblem); and the $10^{-5}$~p.u. slack on the renewable availability bound lowers the objective by about 7.8~k\euro{} at first order, $1.2 \times 10^{-5}$ of it.
